    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d136">

                <pb facs="0227" n="145" xml:id="pg145"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 296/852: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Herrick</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-01-14T12:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 14,
                                1925—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received January 14—10:48
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>43. L–315 [from Herrick, Kellogg and Logan]. Reference Department’s L–196.<anchor
                        n="31" xml:id="d136-f1"/> Subparagraph 2 (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)
                    our L–312 (see our L–312)<anchor n="32" xml:id="d136-f2"/> provides specifically
                    “that the Agreement of May 25, 1923” (in other words specifically the Wadsworth
                    Agreement) “does not apply to payments on account of reparation by any ex-enemy
                    powers other than Germany”. Will be seen by this that only waiver we have made
                    is waiver under Wadsworth Agreement for claims such as example the Bulgarian
                        moneys<anchor n="33" xml:id="d136-f3"/> and in no way affects the rights we
                    have under our treaties with Austria, Hungary and Turkey. Herrick, Kellogg,
                    Logan.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
